<h2>An image is not a complete physical definition</h2>
<p>A rendering or AI-generated image can communicate form, materials, atmosphere, and visual intent. However, it may not clearly define:</p>
<ul>
<li>Exact scale and proportion</li>
<li>Relationships outside the selected view</li>
<li>Circulation and site context</li>
<li>Components and connections</li>
<li>Assembly, transportation, or installation</li>
<li>Whether the geometry works from every viewpoint</li>
</ul>
<p>A convincing image can therefore be a useful starting point without being a fully resolved physical design.</p>

<h2>What does physical verification help clarify?</h2>
<p>Depending on the project, a physical model, sample, or mockup can help a team evaluate:</p>
<ul>
<li>Form, massing, and proportion</li>
<li>Site and spatial relationships</li>
<li>Materials, colors, and finishes</li>
<li>Components and assembly</li>
<li>Lighting or functional features</li>
<li>Handling and installation</li>
<li>How clearly an audience understands the design</li>
</ul>
<p>The model should be built around the question it needs to answer. A massing model, presentation model, material sample, and functional prototype provide different kinds of evidence.</p>

<h2>A shared reference for different audiences</h2>
<p>Architects, developers, contractors, investors, approval agencies, buyers, and community members do not all interpret drawings or digital models in the same way.</p>
<p>A physical model gives them a shared reference. People can move around it, point to the same relationships, compare options, and discuss specific concerns without using specialist software.</p>

<h2>The right evidence—not the most detail</h2>
<p>Physical verification does not require every detail to be reproduced.</p>
<p>A planning model may emphasize height, setbacks, circulation, and context. A sales-gallery model may require developed façades, landscaping, lighting, and finishes. A product prototype may focus on form, assembly, or function.</p>
<p>The appropriate detail depends on what the audience needs to understand or decide.</p>

<h2>Digital and physical tools work together</h2>
<p>Physical models do not replace drawings, BIM, renderings, or AI-generated references. They translate selected design information into a form that can be examined in real space.</p>
<p>The process can be understood as:</p>
<div class="callout" style="text-align:center;font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.98rem;">Digital design → physical interpretation → shared evaluation → informed revision or approval</div>

<h2>From possibility to evidence</h2>
<p>As design becomes easier to generate, evaluating and communicating the right option becomes more important.</p>
<p>A physical model turns selected design information into tangible evidence. It helps teams identify questions, build shared understanding, and move forward with greater confidence.</p>
